/**

\addtogroup pixelwise

\section pixelwise-intro Introduction

Data classes (images, sequences, slices, patches) support a variety of operations,
including arithmetic operations, element-wise functions, and reduction operations.
These operations can be performed on entire arrays or on specific subregions defined by slices or patches.

\section pixelwise-arithmetic Arithmetic operations

Arithmetic operations are provided to the data classes by the `ArithmeticMixin` base class.
Depending on the type of element, `ArithmeticMixin` implements either Boolean or Euclid-space arithmetics.

\section pixelwise-functions Element-wise functions

Many mathematical functions are implemented in the parent class `MathFunctionsMixin`.
Class methods act on the elements in place, while free functions create new instances:

```cpp
auto a = Linx::Image(...);
a.exp(); // Modify `a` elements
auto b = Linx::exp(a); // Create `b`, do not modify `a`
```

When the functions expect parameters, scalars or containers of compatible shapes are accepted:

```cpp
a.pow(b);
a.pow(2);
auto c = Linx::pow(a, b);
auto d = Linx::pow(a, 2);
```

Custom functions can also be provided, either to `generate()` or `apply()`.
They take as input the function to be applied (e.g. free function, functor or lambda)
and an arbitrary number of inputs (can be 0).

For example, to compute `a * k + b`, do:

```cpp
a.apply(KOKKOS_LAMBDA(a_i, b_i) { return a_i * k * b_i}, b);
a.run(Linx::Multiply(k), Linx::Add(b));
```

This is equivalent but much faster than doing simply:

```cpp
a = a * k + b;
```

because intermediate variable `tmp = a * k` is not instanciated.
This is all the more true for formulae with more operations.

This can be seen as an equivalent to NumPy's, XTensor's or Eigen's lazy evaluations, yet with arbitrary functions.

For application of `generate()` and `apply()` to random noise, see \ref random.

\section pixelwise-reduction Reduction operations

*/
